\chapter{Explicit Context-Free Grammars and Earley
Parsing}\label{ch36-explicit-context-free-grammars-and-earley-parsing}

\texttt{CFG} separates grammar productions from lexing. A user lexer
produces a sequence of string tokens; named productions describe how
those tokens form an AST. The Earley parser handles left recursion,
nullable productions and unordered alternatives.

\section{Symbols and productions}\label{ch36-symbols-and-productions}

\texttt{CFGSymbol} distinguishes a terminal token from a nonterminal
name. \texttt{CFGRule} stores a left-hand-side name and a
right-hand-side symbol sequence. \texttt{CFG} stores \texttt{start} and
\texttt{productions} and offers \texttt{add}, \texttt{validate} and
\texttt{parse}.

\begin{code}
dsl::CFG grammar;
grammar.start = "Expr";
grammar.add("Expr", {dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal("Expr"),
                     dsl::CFGSymbol::token("+"),
                     dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal("Term")});
grammar.add("Expr", {dsl::CFGSymbol::nonterminal("Term")});
grammar.add("Term", {dsl::CFGSymbol::token("number")});
grammar.validate();
assert(grammar.parse({"number", "+", "number"}).ok());
\end{code}

\begin{code}
CFG grammar;
grammar.start = "Expr";
grammar.add("Expr", [CFGSymbol.nonterminal("Expr"),
                     CFGSymbol.token("+"), CFGSymbol.nonterminal("Term")]);
grammar.add("Expr", [CFGSymbol.nonterminal("Term")]);
grammar.add("Term", [CFGSymbol.token("number")]);
grammar.validate();
assert(grammar.parse(["number", "+", "number"]).ok());
\end{code}

Terminals compare exactly against string tokens. \texttt{"number"} in
this example is a literal token value supplied by the lexer; it is not a
built-in token class or regex. The user's lexer can normalize numeric
input to that token, or construct a grammar using its own concrete token
values.

Validation requires nonempty production names, a defined start symbol,
and definitions for every referenced nonterminal. \texttt{parse}
validates before running.

\section{Read the grammar from
S-expressions}\label{ch36-read-the-grammar-from-s-expressions}

\begin{code}
(grammar
    (start Expr)
    (rule Expr (Expr "+" Term) (Term))
    (rule Term ("number")))
\end{code}

\begin{code}
auto grammar = dsl::sexpr_grammar(R"((grammar
    (start Expr)
    (rule Expr (Expr "+" Term) (Term))
    (rule Term ("number"))))");
\end{code}

\begin{code}
auto grammar = sexpr_grammar(`(grammar
    (start Expr)
    (rule Expr (Expr "+" Term) (Term))
    (rule Term ("number")))`);
\end{code}

The declaration grammar is:

\begin{code}
grammar = "(grammar" , start , { rule } , ")" ;
start = "(start" , name , ")" ;
rule = "(rule" , name , alternative , { alternative } , ")" ;
alternative = "(" , { symbol } , ")" ;
symbol = nonterminal_name | quoted_terminal ;
\end{code}

The notation permits declaration ordering independent of references: all
references are validated after construction. Unquoted RHS atoms are
nonterminals; quoted RHS atoms are terminals. Each RHS list is a
separate alternative. An empty list is an epsilon production.

\section{Use template-level
vocabulary}\label{ch36-use-template-level-vocabulary}

\texttt{CFGTerminal\textless{}\allowbreak{}Text\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{CFGNonterminal\textless{}\allowbreak{}Name\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and
\texttt{CFGProduction\textless{}\allowbreak{}LHS,\allowbreak{} Symbols.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
provide C++ template construction. D uses native string arguments and
\texttt{!}.

\begin{code}
using Number = dsl::CFGProduction<"Term", dsl::CFGTerminal<"number">>;
using Expression = dsl::CFGProduction<"Expr", dsl::CFGNonterminal<"Term">>;
auto grammar = dsl::make_cfg<"Expr">(Expression{}, Number{});
\end{code}

\begin{code}
alias Number = CFGProduction!("Term", CFGTerminal!"number");
alias Expression = CFGProduction!("Expr", CFGNonterminal!"Term");
auto grammar = make_cfg!"Expr"(Expression(), Number());
\end{code}

\texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}cfg} materializes and validates a
\texttt{CFG}; recognition still runs over runtime tokens. A production
with no symbol types represents epsilon.

\section{Recognition and result ASTs}\label{ch36-recognition-and-result-asts}

Earley chart processing uses prediction, scanning and completion. An
item records its production, dot position and origin token position.
Equivalent chart items are deduplicated, allowing recursive and nullable
grammars to reach a finite chart closure. A completed item carries the
first discovered tree.

\begin{code}
auto parsed = grammar.parse({"number"});
assert(parsed.ok());
assert(parsed.tree->tag() == "Expr");
\end{code}

\begin{code}
auto parsed = grammar.parse(["number"]);
assert(parsed.ok());
assert(parsed.tree.get.tag() == "Expr");
\end{code}

\texttt{CFGParseResult} contains an optional \texttt{tree}, a token
\texttt{position}, expected terminal strings, and
\texttt{ok(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. On success, \texttt{position}
equals the input token count. Production names become inner-node tags;
terminal leaves use tag \texttt{token} and carry the token's text. No
implicit semantic evaluation occurs.

For ambiguous grammars, the parser returns the first discovered
derivation. It does not expose a packed forest of all parses. Use
grammar design and production order deliberately when the selected
derivation matters.

\section{Nullable productions and
diagnostics}\label{ch36-nullable-productions-and-diagnostics}

\begin{code}
(grammar
    (start S)
    (rule S (A A))
    (rule A () ("a")))
\end{code}

This grammar accepts zero, one or two \texttt{a} tokens. The parser
handles completion of nullable symbols even when an appropriate waiting
item is inserted after the nullable completion was first discovered.

For the expression grammar above, input \texttt{number +} reaches token
position 2 and expects terminal \texttt{number}. \texttt{expected}
contains distinct terminal names from the farthest populated chart.
Trailing input can report
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}end-of-input\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
when no ordinary terminal expectation explains it. Undefined grammar
symbols raise a validation exception; token recognition failure is a
result value.

