The same S-expression substrate can hold either equality rules or CFG
declarations, but their readers and semantics are separate.
\texttt{sexpr\_\allowbreak{}rules} reads rewrite rules;
\texttt{sexpr\_\allowbreak{}grammar} reads grammar declarations. A
static byte regex (Chapter 33) can assist a user lexer; it is not a CFG
nonterminal.

\section{Assemble the language
pipeline}\label{ch36-assemble-the-language-pipeline}

A practical progression through the toolkit is:

\begin{enumerate}
\def\labelenumi{\arabic{enumi}.}
\tightlist
\item
  Read source through a user I/O backend.
\item
  Preprocess rule/grammar source and resolve user includes or
  Lua-generated text.
\item
  Read the grammar and tokenize the domain source with the user's lexer.
\item
  Recognize tokens and obtain a tagged AST.
\item
  Derive a visitor for domain validation, evaluation or lowering.
\item
  Convert an expression AST to an e-graph and saturate S-expression
  equalities.
\item
  Trigger domain rewriters on equality and extract a representative.
\item
  Serialize the resulting representation with the user's codec/output
  backend.
\end{enumerate}

The reusable stages are documented in Chapters 32--35; domain
vocabulary, lexing, semantic operations and backend syntax are supplied
by the user.
